      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Judges may perceive urban environments as higher-risk due to sociological factors often linked to higher crime rates and denser social networks involved in illicit activities. Criminological theories sometimes suggest that concentrated disadvantage and proximity to drug markets can increase exposure to criminal elements. However, legally, judges must base decisions on individualized risk assessments, meaning this environmental factor alone is rarely a deciding factor without other aggravating circumstances. Therefore, this perception is often a broad societal bias rather than a strict legal standard for sentencing adjustments.",
